have you ever struggled with the  pronunciation of a foreign language  learning the pronunciation of any  foreign language for that matter is a  little bit challenging a little bit  tricky for a number of reasons but there  are some languages which are  particularly difficult to pronounce  depending on your mother tongue for  example it's really difficult for a  native English speaker to pronounce  languages such as French or Russian or  Arabic and for the rest of the world  English the very same language I am  speaking right now is quite difficult to  pronounce hi my name is Luca from Luca  vampire comm and today I'm going to talk  to you about how to tackle and how to  learn the pronunciation of any language  and in particular of difficult languages  a few months ago I decided to learn a  beautiful Scandinavian language Danish  now if you've never heard Danish Danish  sounds like this just give me the insane  food you get me oh this could be  handsome  yeah that's right it sounds like they  have a big potato in their mouth and  that's what actually other Scandinavians  and other people say about them in  general every language that you learn  will have a different inventory phonetic  inventory that is the amount of sounds  that are contained in the language and  this inventory is different from your  inventory the inventory of your own  native language that's why it is  difficult to acquire the pronunciation  of any other language but there is a key  point to take into account 10 or maybe a  little bit more than 10 years ago I  started learning swedish and like every  other language I just like God in the  language I was listening I was reading I  was doing a lot of things I was exposing  myself to the language and then I made a  youtube video my first youtube video  where I also spoke Swedish and I got you  know some feedback about my  pronunciation and intonation and it  turned out both turned out to be off and  I started asking myself what's wrong  what did I do wrong  well I neglected the fact that Swedish  is a see my tonal language and has pitch  accent so that's a very peculiar feature  of Swedish just to give you an idea you  can have a word that can be pronounced  in two completely different ways for  example you can say on then which means  duck or on then which means spirit and I  had completely overlooked this aspect of  the language as a consequence of that I  developed wrong fanatic patterns and a  few years down the line I just figured  out that my patterns were actually wrong  now if you figured that out that's great  but the problem is that figuring it out  and make it  happen that is fixing or reducing your  accent as someone says is quite  difficult to do  imagine a knot the tighter the knot is  and the better it is  if this knot is tied well but if the  knot is tied wrong and you have to untie  it that's quite a lot of work to do and  most of the time of the game is actually  not worth a candle okay  now you know that you have to start  head-on you have to start from the very  beginning and take care of your  pronunciation and your intonation but in  any language there are some sounds which  are particularly difficult to pronounce  for example the French uh-huh or the  Italian Spanish yeah or the Iying I  cannot pronounce that I don't speak  Arabic but you get the idea and in  Danish you have the infamous soft D for  example when I was in Paris I met a  Danish girl who had me try actually  pronounce a whole call Malou I still  don't pronounce it well maybe a little  bit better and I could not by looking at  her face I could not tell what kind of  sound that was how you could produce it  and when I started learning Danish I  tried my best at looking at some  explanations how do you put your mouth  and I was reading instructions and here  and there and I was hearing the sound  and I was trying to reproduce it until I  stumbled upon a video in this video this  Danish guy makes the case that in order  to pronounce the soft D you have to  start from a given position of your  mouth that you already used in your own  native language and move from there so  just to give an example what he did is  he said you can watch the video by  clicking on the link and the description  box what he does is very simple he says  in order to say oh look the soft D which  actually sounds a little bit like an L  try to start from  the position of the L in your mouth so  if you see L you will see that try  yourself now you will see that your  tongue goes up touches the palate look  this is the way you pronounce L in  English let an Italian slightly  different but the concept is exactly the  same so we have a tendency in our own  native language if your native language  is English or even Spanish or Italian to  pronounce the L by putting the tongue on  an our palate but in Danish that doesn't  happen you have to put VL you have to  keep your tongue down and it's difficult  so at the beginning when I was trying to  say 'la the Consul Amy came up when you  went up the roof of my mouth look look  look and then I told myself you know how  do you how do you sold how do you solve  this and then the guy happened so he  said take two fingers and put the  fingers on your tongue so you keep it  down so uh-huh  this way I know it could be disgusting  but look the hole hole a hole coal so  you try and then you try a few times and  then it actually it happens it it causes  your mind or and your muscle memory to  get accustomed to this new configuration  and then you all you have to do is to  keep going and then you don't need these  fingers anymore so like I started like  this whole whole the whole difficult to  pronounce because I have two fingers in  my mouth but then they started saying it  and I started liking saying it for  example in Danish you say already you  say Allah Allah Allah so whole called  with flu al availa and truth be told I  am sure that I still do not pronounce it  correctly but it was a huge improvement  and I was quite frustrated at the  beginning this goes to show that if you  put your mind to it if you know how to  learn sounds if you use a combination of  channels which is all your channel  visual explanations but also you learn  how to lever  the positions of your mouth when it  comes to producing sounds in your own  language in order to produce other  sounds you can accomplish anything you  just need to know where to start from  and how  learning to pronounce to actually  produce and articulate sounds in your  mouth is fantastic you can be  contraindicated happens when you  actually start producing sentences  because the truth is that we communicate  through messages that are coded in  sentences so when you learn the  pronunciation of a foreign language and  make sure that after some training with  single sounds you take care of how you  pronounce an utter entire sentences and  the example of the soft D when I started  doing is I started saying very short  sentences that chain words that contain  D and I kept saying them over and over  this allowed me to learn the  articulation of the Softee but at the  same time learn the intonation in  sentences remember that we communicate  in messages these messages are coded in  chunks and learning in chunks is much  faster and more efficient than learning  the single components of the language  okay this is the end of the video  unfortunately but here is the wrap-up  first make sure that you tackle  pronunciation and intonation the  phonetic fabric of a language head on  from the very beginning to get patterns  right because if you do that your life  will get much easier down the line  second make sure that when it comes to  particularly difficult sounds to  pronounce you actually train and you try  to figure out how to pronounce these  languages not just by sound not just by  hearing not just by looking and  explanations but also to see visually  how people pronounce how native  pronounce e sounds and whether it's  possible just to move from a position of  your mouth that you already know and you  already used in your native language or  a language you speak and third make sure  that you move from training on single  sounds to training on short fragments or  sentences or phrases and you can kill  the proverbial two birds with one stone  now I got a challenge for you and some  more info about this topic if you have a  camera and if you have a good microphone  and make a video of yourself trying to  train on a specific sound and trying to  say a few fragments or a few phrases a  few sentences using that sound so you  have a camera make a video of yourself  training on a specific sound that you  find difficult and remember to train not  only on single words that contain the  sound but also on short sentences and if  you want to know more please click on  the link in the description box I have  written a very extensive article about  this very same topic that you can read  and listen to and of course drop a bunch  of comments in the comment section below  thank you very much and see you soon on  YouTube  [Music]

